#!/bin/bash
# post-commit hook — DISABLED, replaced by CI (see issue #627)
#
# This hook used to increment .version and amend the commit. It could not
# work under the PR-only workflow, and had been silently doing nothing for
# some time: `main` sat at 1.5.0 across several `feat:` merges that each
# should have bumped MINOR.
#
# Why it was unreachable, in both directions:
#
#   1. It exited early unless the current branch was `main`. Every commit is
#      authored on a fix/* or dev/* branch, so that check exited 0.
#   2. The commit that actually lands on `main` is created by GitHub's
#      squash-merge, server-side, where no local hook runs at all.
#
# So there was no point at which the old logic could fire. Amending was also
# wrong for this workflow independently: it would rewrite a commit that has
# already been pushed to `main`.
#
# The replacement is `.github/workflows/version-bump.yml`, which computes the
# bump from the PR TITLE (the string that becomes the squash-merge commit
# subject) and pushes a `chore(version): bump to X.Y.Z` commit onto the PR's
# own branch — so squash-merge lands it as part of the same change. A
# "Version Check" job in the same workflow fails the PR if `.version`, the
# OpenAPI `info.version`, and the version embedded in `api/api.go` ever drift
# out of step. See CLAUDE.md's "Versioning" section and #627 for the full
# design.
#
# Deliberately kept as an explanatory no-op rather than deleted, so the
# history is discoverable from the path the docs point at, and so
# `make install-hooks` (which chmods this file) keeps working.
#
# scripts/update-version.sh and scripts/ci-version-bump.sh are left in place;
# the latter is what the CI workflow actually calls, the former still works
# for a one-off manual/local bump.

exit 0
